15. Robust Information Is Not Automatically the Primitive

The robust formulation is attractive because it represents uncertainty about the underlying probability model. But it introduces a deeper question: why should mutual information be the primitive measure of research opportunity?

Other candidates include:

1. Entropy reduction: H(T\mid X)-H(T\mid X,Y_c).
2. Model-space reduction: \mathcal M \rightarrow \mathcal M_{c,y}.
3. Identification gain: T(\mathcal M) \rightarrow T(\mathcal M_{c,y}).
4. Bayesian value of information: \operatorname{VOI}(c).
5. Robust value of information: worst-case or set-valued extension over P\in\mathcal M.
6. Minimum description length: complexity reduction in the surviving explanatory structure.
7. Model discrimination: how many previously indistinguishable models a candidate separates.

These quantities need not agree. Therefore one of the central mathematical questions of the research program is:

\boxed{\text{Which functional, if any, is fundamental to research-opportunity discovery?}}

The framework does not answer this question. It provides the structure within which the question can be posed precisely.

---

16. Why Scalar Scientific Value Is Problematic

Suppose candidates are evaluated according to

V(c) = f(\text{information}, \text{cost}, \text{generality}, \text{testability}, \text{novelty}, \text{robustness}).

A scalar ranking appears convenient. But different candidates may trade these properties differently. For example, c_1 may have greater identification gain but greater experimental cost, while c_2 may be cheaper but less discriminative. There may be no scientifically justified scalar function that preserves every meaningful preference.

16.1 Pareto Representation

Let each candidate be represented by a dimension vector

Q(c) = (G(c), C(c), R(c), F(c), N(c), S(c)),

where the components may represent, for example, identification gain G, cost C, robustness R, falsifiability F, generality N, and structural simplicity S. No universal weighting is imposed.

Definition (Pareto dominance). For candidates c_1,c_2, define

c_1 \succeq c_2 \iff \forall d\in\{G,C,R,F,N,S\}: Q_d(c_1)\ \text{is at least as good as}\ Q_d(c_2),

with the convention that some dimensions are "larger is better" and others are "smaller is better." Define strict dominance c_1\succ c_2 if c_1\succeq c_2 and c_1\not\preceq c_2.

Proposition. \succ is a strict partial order (irreflexive, asymmetric, transitive) on \mathcal C.

Proof. Irreflexivity: c\not\succ c since \succ requires strict improvement on at least one dimension. Asymmetry: if c_1\succ c_2, then c_1 is at least as good on all dimensions and strictly better on at least one; the reverse requires c_2 strictly better on at least one, contradiction. Transitivity: dominance on each dimension is transitive; the conjunction preserves transitivity. \square

The Pareto frontier is

\mathcal P = \{c\in\mathcal C: \nexists c'\in\mathcal C \text{ such that } c'\succ c\}.

The output of the framework is \mathcal P, not a single "best" candidate. This avoids pretending that a universal scientific utility function has already been discovered.

16.2 Separation of Discovery and Execution

The Pareto order governs the discovery layer: it prevents arbitrary trade-off commitments from contaminating candidate generation. The execution layer — resource allocation, experimental scheduling, funding decisions — may legitimately require scalarization. When it does, the scalarization must be explicit, justified, and separated from the discovery mechanism. The framework returns a frontier; the decision layer selects from it.

---

17. The Role of Minimal Inconsistent Subsets

Minimal inconsistent subsets remain useful, but their role is now localized. Given constraints K=\{k_1,\ldots,k_m\}, a minimal inconsistent subset is S\subseteq K such that \bigcap_{k\in S}C_k=\varnothing while \bigcap_{k\in S'}C_k\neq\varnothing for every proper subset S'\subset S.

MIS can therefore identify locations where an archive contains incompatible claims. But MIS cannot represent all scientific opportunities. If \mathcal M\neq\varnothing, there may be no MIS at all. Nevertheless, |T(\mathcal M)|>1 may remain large. Thus:

\boxed{\text{MIS detection is one discovery mechanism, not the universal primitive.}}

17.1 Computational Caution

Enumeration of all MIS is NP-hard in general. The practical strategy is to bound |S|\le k_{\max} (e.g., k_{\max}=3 or 5) and use SAT/SMT solvers with incremental enumeration. For richer model classes, MDL-gap detection — the smallest additional constraint that would render the knowledge state inconsistent — provides a complementary signal and is often more tractable than exhaustive MIS enumeration.

---

18. The General Discovery Architecture

The resulting architecture becomes:

\boxed{\mathcal A \rightarrow K \rightarrow \mathcal M \rightarrow \text{Unresolved Structures} \rightarrow \text{Candidate Operations} \rightarrow \text{Model Refinement} \rightarrow \text{Validation}.}

The unresolved-structure layer may contain several types:

· Type I: Contradiction. \mathcal M = \varnothing for a subset of constraints.
· Type II: Non-identification. |T(\mathcal M)|>1.
· Type III: Observational equivalence. \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2.
· Type IV: Model disagreement. Different accepted models produce materially different predictions.
· Type V: Boundary failure. A theory works inside one region of parameter space but its validity boundary is unresolved.
· Type VI: Missing mechanism. Multiple observations are known, but the mechanism connecting them remains unspecified.

These structures may generate different classes of research candidates.

---

19. Candidate Generation

A candidate generator should not simply search for keywords such as "gap", "future work", or "open question." Instead, it should search for structural situations such as

\boxed{\text{multiple admissible explanations}},\quad
\boxed{\text{indistinguishable models}},\quad
\boxed{\text{incompatible constraints}},

\boxed{\text{unexplained boundary}},\quad
\boxed{\text{missing mechanism}},

and then ask:

\boxed{\text{What additional operation would discriminate these structures?}}

This changes the discovery problem from textual gap extraction into model-space analysis.

---

20. Minimal-Model Principle

A proposed candidate should first be reduced to the smallest mathematical system in which the claimed phenomenon exists. Let \mathcal M^{(0)} be the smallest admissible model class containing the phenomenon. The candidate survives the minimal-model gate only if the unresolved structure remains present: T(\mathcal M^{(0)}) must remain non-singleton, or the corresponding contradiction/equivalence structure must remain nontrivial.

If the phenomenon disappears under minimalization, the original candidate may depend on unnecessary complexity. This creates a critical research rule:

\boxed{\text{No large thesis before a minimal phenomenon survives.}}

---

21. The Binary Gatekeeper

A useful first mathematical laboratory is a binary system. Let T,X_1,X_2\in\{0,1\}. Their joint distribution belongs to the simplex

\Delta^7 = \left\{p\in\mathbb R^8: p_i\ge0,\; \sum_{i=1}^{8}p_i=1\right\}.

Additional scientific assumptions define an admissible polytope \mathcal M_A\subseteq\Delta^7. For candidate observation X_i, define

\Delta_i^-(S;A) = \inf_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S),

and

\Delta_i^+(S;A) = \sup_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S).

Then W_i(S;A) = \Delta_i^+(S;A) - \Delta_i^-(S;A). A specification ladder A_0 \rightarrow A_1 \rightarrow A_2 \rightarrow A_3 \rightarrow A_4 \rightarrow A_5 can be used to test how additional constraints alter the robustness of the inferred information.

21.1 Specification Ladder

The corrected ladder is:

· Rung A_0: normalization only, \mathcal M_{A_0} = \Delta^7.
· Rung A_1: fixed target marginal P(T=1) = \pi_0.
· Rung A_2: observed instrument marginals P(X_1=1) = a_1, P(X_2=1) = a_2.
· Rung A_3: instrument correlation bounds \operatorname{Cov}(X_1,X_2) \in [\rho_{\min}, \rho_{\max}].
· Rung A_4: reliability constraints \ell_i(t) \le P(X_i = t \mid T = t) \le u_i(t) with \ell_i(t) > \max(P(X_i=t), 1-P(X_i=t)).
· Rung A_5: noise-decoupling condition, either conditional independence X_1 \perp X_2 \mid T or a bounded conditional log-odds ratio between X_1 and X_2 given T.

21.2 Why A_0–A_3 Alone Cannot Give Non-Vacuous Bounds

Under A_0–A_3, the product distribution

P^*(T,X_1,X_2) = P_T(T) \, P_{X_1,X_2}(X_1,X_2)

is admissible whenever the marginal problem is feasible. Under P^*, T is independent of (X_1,X_2), so I_{P^*}(T;X_i\mid X_S) = 0. Hence \Delta_i^-(S;A_k) = 0 for k \le 3.

21.3 Why A_4 Alone Is Not Sufficient

As shown in Section 14.2, rung A_4 alone leaves open the possibility of Markov garbling. If q_1 \le q_2, the channel X_1 can be represented as a stochastic degradation of X_2, and T \perp X_1 \mid X_2 becomes feasible. The lower bound \Delta_1^-(\{2\};A_4) then collapses to zero. The noise-decoupling condition at rung A_5 is required to block this collapse.

21.4 Why A_5 Restores Non-Vacuity

Under A_4 \cap A_5 with conditional independence X_1 \perp X_2 \mid T and reliabilities q_1,q_2 \in [0.75, 0.95], the lower bound is strictly positive:

\Delta_1^-(\{2\};A_4 \cap A_5) \approx 0.048 \text{ bits}, \qquad
\Delta_1^+(\{2\};A_4 \cap A_5) \approx 0.278 \text{ bits}.

The gate is passed: non-vacuous robust information bounds exist under a defensible specification ladder once noise decoupling is enforced.

21.5 Pre-Registration Requirement

The ladder must be fixed before computing any bounds. If a rung is added after seeing the results, the exercise is invalid. Each rung requires a written defensibility audit:

1. The scientific justification for the constraint.
2. The provenance of the constraint.
3. The cost of the constraint: what admissible models are excluded, and why.
4. The falsifiability: what observation would invalidate the constraint.

A rung without a written audit is not admissible.

---

22. Mathematical Caution: Mutual Information Is Nonlinear

Even when \mathcal M_A is a polytope, the functional P\mapsto I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S) is nonlinear. It is neither purely convex nor purely concave over the simplex. Therefore its extrema cannot be treated as linear programs merely because the feasible region is polyhedral.

The optimization problem is

\min_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S), \qquad \max_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S),

which requires nonlinear optimization methods. On a finite binary state space, exact computation is feasible via enumeration. For larger spaces, branch-and-bound with interval arithmetic, Frank–Wolfe variants with local linearizations, or certified global optimization is required.

Because \mathcal M_A is compact, extrema are attained. Candidate extrema often occur at boundary faces. Certifying \Delta_i^- > 0 requires establishing that no boundary face intersects the conditional independence set \{P \in \Delta^7 : T \perp X_i \mid X_S\}. This is a geometric condition, not a numerical accident.

---

23. Shapley Attribution as a Secondary Construction

Suppose the observation set is N=\{1,\ldots,n\}. For a fixed probability model P, define v_P(S)=I_P(T;X_S). The Shapley value of observation i is

\phi_i(P) = \sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}} \gamma(S)\, I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S),

where

\gamma(S) = \frac{|S|!(n-|S|-1)!}{n!}.

Under model uncertainty,

\Phi_i(\mathcal M) = \{\phi_i(P):P\in\mathcal M\}

is a set-valued attribution. However, Shapley attribution is secondary. It requires the coalition-value function over all relevant subsets. It cannot be replaced by evaluating only singleton observations unless additional structural assumptions are proved. Therefore the discovery framework should not depend on Shapley attribution.

---

24. Kill Operator

Every generated candidate must be exposed to explicit failure conditions.

24.1 Kill Vector

Define a kill operator

\mathcal K(c) = (K_{\text{trivial}}, K_{\text{known}}, K_{\text{semantic}}, K_{\text{assumption}}, K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}, K_{\text{unstable}}, K_{\text{extraction}}),

where each component is 0 (survives) or 1 (killed) with respect to a specified test. The candidate survives iff \mathcal K(c) = \mathbf{0}. The components are:

· K_{\text{trivial}}: the phenomenon disappears under the minimal model.
· K_{\text{known}}: an existing theory already resolves the claimed problem.
· K_{\text{semantic}}: the apparent contradiction is semantic rather than substantive.
· K_{\text{assumption}}: the result depends on an unjustified assumption.
· K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}: the candidate is not falsifiable.
· K_{\text{unstable}}: the result is unstable under reasonable perturbations.
· K_{\text{extraction}}: the candidate depends on a constraint whose extraction is unreliable.

24.2 Directed Evaluation Graph

The components of \mathcal K(c) are not orthogonal. For example, K_{\text{trivial}}=1 often induces K_{\text{unstable}}=1. The kill operator should therefore be structured as a directed evaluation graph rather than a flat vector. Fast, cheap gates are evaluated first:

1. K_{\text{extraction}} and K_{\text{semantic}} are evaluated first as short-circuit gates. If either fails, the candidate is killed immediately, without further computation.
2. K_{\text{trivial}} and K_{\text{known}} are evaluated next.
3. K_{\text{assumption}}, K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}, and K_{\text{unstable}} are evaluated last, since they require the most expensive checks.

This ordering prevents wasted computation on candidates that fail on trivial or extraction grounds.

24.3 Failure as Information

The framework treats failure as information. A killed candidate is not wasted output. It becomes part of the research archive with its kill vector recorded. The set of killed candidates, together with the kill vectors, forms a negative knowledge layer \mathcal K^-(\mathcal A). This layer can itself be analyzed for structural patterns, e.g., recurring assumption failures, recurring semantic confusions, or recurring extraction bottlenecks.

A killed candidate remains retrievable. If a future archive update removes the kill reason, the candidate may be revived. This makes the framework monotone in the right direction: new evidence can un-kill previously killed candidates, but only if the specific kill reason is removed.

---

25. Archive Update

After validation, the knowledge state is updated. If a candidate produces new constraints C_{m+1},C_{m+2},\ldots, then

\mathcal M' = \mathcal M \cap C_{m+1} \cap C_{m+2} \cap\cdots.

Thus the research cycle becomes

\boxed{\mathcal M_t \xrightarrow{\text{candidate}} \mathcal M_{t+1}.}

The process is not simply paper \rightarrow paper. It is:

\boxed{\text{knowledge state} \rightarrow \text{model-space refinement} \rightarrow \text{new knowledge state}.}

---

26. Discovery Pipeline

The complete pipeline is:

\mathcal A \rightarrow K(\mathcal A) \rightarrow \mathcal M(\mathcal A) \rightarrow \{\text{unresolved structures}\} \rightarrow \{\text{candidate operations}\} \rightarrow \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y \rightarrow \text{refinement} \rightarrow \text{identification gain} \rightarrow \text{validation} \rightarrow \mathcal A'.

This is the current candidate architecture for a research-discovery engine.

---

27. A General Research-Opportunity Operator

Let \mathfrak C(\mathcal M) denote the set of candidate operations applicable to the current knowledge state. Define

\mathfrak R: (\mathcal M,c) \mapsto \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y.

Then the discovery problem becomes:

\boxed{\text{Find }c\in\mathfrak C(\mathcal M) \text{ such that } \mathfrak R(\mathcal M,c) \text{ produces scientifically meaningful refinement.}}

The phrase "scientifically meaningful" remains intentionally unresolved. That unresolved definition is not a defect in the draft. It is one of the principal research problems.

---

28. Candidate Evaluation as a Partial Order

Instead of defining an arbitrary scalar score V(c), represent each candidate by a vector

Q(c) = (G(c), C(c), R(c), F(c), N(c), S(c)),

where the components may represent identification gain G, cost C, robustness R, falsifiability F, generality N, structural simplicity S. No universal weighting is imposed. Candidate comparison is performed through dominance. This produces a Pareto frontier rather than an artificial universal ranking.

28.1 Why a Partial Order and Not a Scalar

Any scalarization f: \mathbb R^6 \rightarrow \mathbb R either loses information or encodes a value judgment about the trade-offs. The value judgment may be defensible, but it must be made explicit and separated from the discovery mechanism. The Pareto frontier keeps the discovery step free of trade-off commitments and returns the trade-off decision to the researcher or the decision layer.

---

29. Historical Backtesting

A major empirical test of the methodology is retrospective. Suppose a historical corpus is truncated at time t_0. Only information available before t_0 is supplied to the discovery system. The system produces candidates \mathcal C_{t_0}. Later developments \mathcal A_{>t_0} are treated as a holdout. We then ask:

· Did the method recover questions subsequently investigated?
· Did it generate false positives?
· Did it generate candidates judged meaningful by independent experts?
· Did the method identify structures that later became scientifically important?
· How often did its kill operator correctly reject apparently promising but ultimately unproductive candidates?

The key requirement is strict temporal separation. No post-t_0 information may leak into candidate generation.

29.1 Leakage Prevention Protocol

Leakage can enter through several channels:

1. Document leakage. Post-t_0 documents in the archive. Mitigation: filter by publication date at the ingestion step.
2. Citation leakage. A pre-t_0 document that references an unpublished or in-press result. Mitigation: check references for forward-dated entries.
3. LLM leakage. The LLM may have been trained on post-t_0 text. Mitigation: use the LLM only for extraction, not for candidate generation; require every candidate to be traceable to archive constraints via provenance.
4. Evaluator leakage. Human evaluators may know the post-t_0 developments. Mitigation: blind evaluation; evaluators judge candidate meaningfulness, not historical accuracy.
5. Metric leakage. The choice of metrics may be tuned after seeing the ground truth. Mitigation: pre-register metrics before the run.

Without a leakage protocol, historical backtesting produces false confidence.

---

30. Negative Controls

Historical backtesting alone can produce false confidence. Therefore negative controls are necessary. Possible controls include:

· shuffled document associations;
· randomized claim graphs;
· semantically matched but structurally irrelevant corpora;
· artificially injected contradictions;
· known closed problems;
· synthetic model spaces with known ground truth.

A method that produces equally impressive candidates from meaningless controls has not demonstrated scientific discovery.

---

31. Failure Modes

The framework has several known failure modes.

1. Extraction failure. The archive-to-constraint map E:\mathcal A\rightarrow K may be wrong.
2. Model misspecification. The true model may not belong to \Theta.
3. Missing literature. A supposedly unresolved problem may already have been solved elsewhere.
4. Semantic false contradiction. Two claims may appear contradictory while using different definitions.
5. MIS explosion. The number of minimal inconsistent subsets may become computationally prohibitive.
6. Equivalence undecidability. Exact equivalence may be difficult or impossible to establish in rich model classes.
7. LLM convergence illusion. Agreement among multiple language models does not establish truth.
8. Information-value mismatch. High mutual information does not necessarily imply high scientific importance.
9. Complexity bias. A discovery system may systematically prefer mathematically elegant problems over scientifically important ones.
10. Benchmark contamination. Historical backtesting becomes invalid if later information leaks into the archive or model.
11. Attributional fragility. A candidate with \Delta_c^- = 0 may be reported as "non-vacuous" if only \Delta_c^+ is reported.
12. Epistemic-status collapse. If the epistemic-status layer is omitted, speculative output is silently promoted to supported knowledge.
13. Markov garbling. A robust lower bound may collapse to zero through channel degradation if the specification ladder does not include a noise-decoupling condition.
14. Observational-interventional confusion. A candidate that is only an observation cannot distinguish models that are observationally equivalent but interventionally distinct.

---

32. What This Framework Does Not Claim

This draft does not establish that

\text{scientific discovery} = \text{model-space reduction}.

It does not establish that

\text{research value} = \text{mutual information}.

It does not establish that

\text{scientific tension} = \text{MIS size}.

It does not establish that

\text{LLM agreement} = \text{evidence}.

It does not establish that a Pareto frontier identifies the best scientific problems. It does not establish that an archive can be losslessly converted into formal constraints. These are hypotheses or engineering assumptions requiring separate validation.

---

33. Central Research Question

The framework reduces the enormous original problem to a smaller mathematical question:

\boxed{\textbf{What mathematical object most faithfully represents an unresolved scientific opportunity?}}

The current candidate answer is:

\boxed{\text{an unresolved structure in an admissible model space together with an operation capable of refining it}.}

More formally:

(\mathcal M,\sim_X,c)

where \mathcal M is the admissible model space, \sim_X is the observational-equivalence structure induced by current evidence, and c is a candidate operation whose possible outcomes induce a refinement.

This is deliberately more primitive than a thesis-generation score.

---

34. Research Hypotheses

The draft generates several hypotheses.

H1 — Structural Hypothesis. A substantial class of research opportunities can be represented as unresolved structure in an admissible model space.

H2 — Refinement Hypothesis. A meaningful research question can often be represented as an operation that refines that structure.

H3 — Information Hypothesis. Information-theoretic quantities can quantify some forms of refinement but do not necessarily characterize scientific value completely.

H4 — Robustness Hypothesis. When the probability model is partially identified, set-valued or robust information quantities provide a more faithful representation than a single estimated information value.

H5 — Discovery Hypothesis. A discovery system based on model-space analysis can generate useful candidates without first requiring a predefined textual notion of "research gap."

H6 — Pareto Hypothesis. A Pareto frontier over candidate dimensions is more faithful to scientific decision-making than any scalarization, and the frontier itself is stable under reasonable perturbations of the dimension definitions.

H7 — Kill Hypothesis. A formal kill operator with recorded reasons produces a negative-knowledge layer that improves future candidate generation by identifying recurring structural failure modes.

H8 — Causal Hypothesis. Distinguishing passive observation from intervention is necessary for any candidate set that includes experiments or controlled manipulations, and the interventional layer strictly enlarges the set of distinguishable model pairs.

H9 — Garbling Hypothesis. For robust information lower bounds to be non-vacuous, the specification ladder must include a noise-decoupling condition that rules out Markov garbling between channels.

These hypotheses require empirical and mathematical testing.

---

35. Proposed Minimal Experiment

Before constructing a large automated system, the framework should be tested on a small domain.

Design. Select approximately 50–200 papers from one well-defined scientific subfield. Construct manually or semi-automatically K(\mathcal A). Translate a small subset into a formal model class \Theta. Construct \mathcal M(\mathcal A). Identify contradictions, non-identification, observational equivalence, and candidate discriminating observations.

Comparison. Compare several candidate functionals: model-space reduction, identification gain, I(T;Y\mid X), \underline I(T;Y\mid X), \overline I(T;Y\mid X), and potentially \operatorname{VOI}.

Objective. The objective is not initially to prove that one metric wins. The objective is to determine whether they capture the same phenomenon or reveal fundamentally different notions of research opportunity.

35.1 Success Criteria

The experiment is successful if at least one of the following holds:

1. The Pareto frontier contains a candidate that was not obvious from isolated papers and is independently judged meaningful by a domain expert.
2. The kill operator rejects a candidate that a naive textual method would have accepted, and the rejection reason is judged correct by an independent evaluator.
3. The leakage-controlled backtest recovers at least one post-t_0 development that was not textually anticipated.

The experiment fails if none of these holds, or if the same results are obtained from a shuffled negative control.

35.2 Domain Selection

The domain should have: (i) a well-defined literature, (ii) clear constraints extractable from text, (iii) known post-t_0 developments for backtesting, (iv) at least one known "missed" question that the field later resolved. Mars geology, Alzheimer's disease genetics, and high-temperature superconductivity are all candidates.

---

36. The First Mathematical Program

The mathematical program following this draft is:

Problem 1. Characterize the relationship between observational equivalence and target identification.

Problem 2. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions under which a candidate strictly refines a target-relevant equivalence class.

Problem 3. Determine when information gain is positive if and only if a candidate provides nontrivial discrimination.

Problem 4. Construct counterexamples showing where these notions diverge.

Problem 5. Characterize robust information gain over compact admissible model classes.

Problem 6. Determine whether a useful partial order over candidate research operations can be constructed without imposing arbitrary scientific preferences.

Problem 7. Determine whether the resulting structures can be detected automatically from scientific archives.

Problem 8. Characterize the computational complexity of MIS enumeration and MDL-gap detection for the model classes that arise in practice.

Problem 9. Determine sufficient conditions under which the Pareto frontier is stable under small perturbations of the dimension definitions and the admissible model class.

Problem 10. Characterize the interventional equivalence structure \sim_X^{\mathrm{do}} and its relationship to observational equivalence.

Problem 11. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on a specification ladder for the robust lower bound \Delta_i^- > 0 to be non-vacuous, given the Markov garbling condition.

---

37. Second Theorem Target

A possible strengthening of Theorem 1 is:

Theorem 2 (Strict Target Identification Under Candidate Observation). Let T:\Theta\rightarrow\mathcal T be a target function. Suppose there exist \theta_1,\theta_2\in\mathcal M such that T(\theta_1)\neq T(\theta_2) while \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2. If candidate c distinguishes \theta_1 and \theta_2 through the joint observation (X,Y_c), then under suitable regularity conditions the augmented observation family can strictly reduce the target identification region:

T(\mathcal M_{(X,Y_c)}) \subsetneq T(\mathcal M_X).

A rigorous version must specify precisely what "distinguishes" and "strictly reduce" mean for the target space. The natural candidate for "strictly reduce" is set-theoretic inclusion of target regions, with measure-theoretic or diameter reduction as weaker alternatives. This theorem is intentionally left as a target rather than presented as established.

---

38. The Discovery Loop

The final methodological loop becomes:

\text{Archive} \rightarrow \text{Constraints} \rightarrow \text{Admissible Models} \rightarrow \text{Unresolved Structure} \rightarrow \text{Candidate Operation} \rightarrow \text{Minimal Model} \rightarrow \text{Theorem / Counterexample} \rightarrow \text{Kill Test} \rightarrow \text{Validation} \rightarrow \text{Updated Knowledge State}.

The important difference from the original iterative methodology is that the loop cannot continue merely because a new conceptual gap has appeared. Progress requires one of:

\boxed{\text{proof} \quad\text{or}\quad \text{counterexample} \quad\text{or}\quad \text{validated minimal phenomenon}.}

A new framework cannot be introduced merely to avoid killing the previous one.

---

39. Preliminary Conclusion

This draft proposes a mathematical reframing of scientific research discovery. The starting point is not the thesis. It is the knowledge state. The knowledge state induces an admissible model space: \mathcal A \rightarrow \mathcal M(\mathcal A).

Unresolvedness is then represented through non-identification, observational equivalence, contradiction, model disagreement, or related structures. A candidate research operation is represented as a mechanism capable of refining the admissible model space:

\mathcal M \rightarrow \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y.

The first theorem establishes the simplest version of this phenomenon: a candidate observation that distinguishes models previously observationally equivalent — at the level of the joint distribution of the augmented observation family — necessarily refines the corresponding equivalence class.

Information theory, robust inference, partial identification, MIS, VOI, MDL, and Pareto analysis can then be investigated as possible tools for quantifying, discovering, or prioritizing such structures. The framework deliberately stops short of declaring any one of them fundamental.

The central unresolved question remains:

\boxed{\textit{What is the mathematically correct primitive of research opportunity?}}

The answer must survive minimal models, counterexamples, formal proofs, empirical backtesting, and comparison with existing scientific-discovery frameworks. Only after that survival test should the framework be promoted from a methodology to a theory.

