Appendix A — Formal Proofs

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Epistemic-Status Propagation is Well-Defined)

Statement. Let (\mathcal S, \preceq) be the graded epistemic-status lattice with

\mathcal S_{\mathrm{graded}} = \{\text{verified}, \text{supported}, \text{hypothetical}, \text{speculative}\}

ordered by

\text{verified} \succ \text{supported} \succ \text{hypothetical} \succ \text{speculative}.

If a claim k is derived from \{k_{j_1},\ldots,k_{j_r}\} with graded statuses \sigma(k_{j_i}), then the induced status

\sigma(k) := \min_{i} \sigma(k_{j_i})

is well-defined and independent of the order of derivation.

Proof. The minimum is taken in the total preorder \preceq. Since \preceq is total on the four graded statuses, the minimum exists and is unique up to equality. Order-independence follows from commutativity of \min in a total preorder. \square

A.2 Terminal Absorbents

Statement. Let \mathcal S_{\mathrm{terminal}} = \{\text{contradicted}, \text{killed}, \text{unresolved}\}. For a derived claim k with dependencies \{k_{j_1},\ldots,k_{j_r}\}, define

\sigma(k) =
\begin{cases}
\text{contradicted} & \text{if } \exists i: \sigma(k_{j_i}) = \text{contradicted} \\
\text{killed} & \text{if } \exists i: \sigma(k_{j_i}) = \text{killed} \text{ and no } j: \sigma(k_{j_j}) = \text{contradicted} \\
\text{unresolved} & \text{if } \exists i: \sigma(k_{j_i}) = \text{unresolved} \text{ and no contradicted/killed dependencies} \\
\min_i \sigma(k_{j_i}) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

where the final minimum is taken over the graded statuses.

Proof. The terminal statuses are absorbents in the derivation graph: once a dependency is contradicted, the derived claim cannot be more secure than contradiction. The priority ordering \text{contradicted} \succ \text{killed} \succ \text{unresolved} \succ \text{graded} ensures well-definedness. The rule is deterministic given the statuses of dependencies. \square

Corollary. A derived claim with at least one contradicted dependency is itself contradicted, regardless of the statuses of its other dependencies.

A.3 Proof of Theorem 1 (Candidate-Induced Refinement)

Statement. Let \Theta be a model space and let X be the current observation family. Define

\theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2 \iff P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B) \quad \forall B.

Let Y_c be the observation generated by candidate c. If \theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2 but

P_{\theta_1}(X\in B, Y_c\in B') \neq P_{\theta_2}(X\in B, Y_c\in B')

for some measurable rectangle B \times B', then \theta_1 \not\sim_{(X,Y_c)} \theta_2, and consequently

[\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} \subsetneq [\theta_1]_X.

Proof. Suppose \theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2. Then for every measurable B \subseteq \mathcal X,

P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B).

By assumption there exists a measurable rectangle B \times B' \subseteq \mathcal X \times \mathcal Y_c such that

P_{\theta_1}((X,Y_c) \in B \times B') \neq P_{\theta_2}((X,Y_c) \in B \times B').

Therefore the joint law of (X,Y_c) differs between \theta_1 and \theta_2, so \theta_1 \not\sim_{(X,Y_c)} \theta_2.

Since \theta_2 \in [\theta_1]_X and \theta_2 \notin [\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)}, we have the set inclusion

[\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} \subseteq [\theta_1]_X.

The inclusion is proper because \theta_2 is in the right-hand side but not the left. \square

Corollary A.3.1 (Marginal Witness). If there exists B' such that P_{\theta_1}(Y_c\in B') \neq P_{\theta_2}(Y_c\in B'), then the hypotheses of Theorem 1 hold with the cylinder event \mathcal X \times B'.

Corollary A.3.2 (No Witness). If no such rectangle exists, then [\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} = [\theta_1]_X for all \theta_1, i.e., candidate c produces no refinement at the level of observational equivalence classes.

A.4 Proof of Proposition 16.1 (Pareto Order is a Strict Partial Order)

Statement. Define c_1 \succeq c_2 iff for all dimensions d \in \{G,C,R,F,N,S\},

Q_d(c_1) \text{ is at least as good as } Q_d(c_2),

with orientation conventions fixed per dimension. Define c_1 \succ c_2 iff c_1 \succeq c_2 and c_2 \not\succeq c_1. Then \succ is irreflexive, asymmetric, and transitive.

Proof.

Irreflexivity. Suppose c \succ c. Then c \succeq c (trivially true) and c \not\succeq c (required for strictness), a contradiction. Hence c \not\succ c.

Asymmetry. Suppose c_1 \succ c_2. Then c_1 \succeq c_2 and c_2 \not\succeq c_1. If also c_2 \succ c_1, then c_2 \succeq c_1, contradicting c_2 \not\succeq c_1. Hence c_2 \not\succ c_1.

Transitivity. Suppose c_1 \succ c_2 and c_2 \succ c_3. Then:

· c_1 \succeq c_2 and c_2 \succeq c_3 imply c_1 \succeq c_3 (dominance is componentwise and hence transitive).
· c_3 \not\succeq c_1 because if c_3 \succeq c_1, then c_3 \succeq c_1 \succeq c_2 \succeq c_3, so c_3 \succeq c_2, contradicting c_2 \succ c_3 (which requires c_3 \not\succeq c_2).

Hence c_1 \succ c_3. \square

Remark. The Pareto frontier \mathcal P = \{c : \nexists c' \text{ with } c' \succ c\} is nonempty whenever \mathcal C is finite and nonempty, by a standard ascending-chain argument on the partial order.

A.5 Proof of Proposition 14.1 (\Delta_c^- = 0 is not equivalent to attributional vacuity)

Statement. Let \Delta_c^- = \inf_{P \in \mathcal M} I_P(T;Y_c \mid X) and \Delta_c^+ = \sup_{P \in \mathcal M} I_P(T;Y_c \mid X). Then:

(a) \Delta_c^- = 0 does not imply that I_P(T;Y_c\mid X) = 0 for all P \in \mathcal M.

(b) \Delta_c^- = 0 is compatible with \Delta_c^+ > 0.

(c) \Delta_c^- > 0 implies I_P(T;Y_c\mid X) > 0 for all P \in \mathcal M.

Proof.

(a) The infimum equals 0 if there exists P_0 \in \mathcal M with I_{P_0}(T;Y_c\mid X) = 0, or if a sequence approaches 0. Either way, other P \in \mathcal M may have positive value.

(b) Choose \mathcal M = \{P_0, P_1\} with I_{P_0} = 0 and I_{P_1} = c > 0. Then \Delta_c^- = 0, \Delta_c^+ = c.

(c) Immediate from definition of infimum and nonnegativity of CMI. \square

A.6 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Compactness of Finite Polytope)

Statement. If \mathcal M_A \subseteq \Delta^{K-1} is defined by finitely many linear equalities and inequalities and is nonempty, then \mathcal M_A is compact.

Proof. The simplex \Delta^{K-1} is closed and bounded in \mathbb R^K, hence compact (Heine–Borel). \mathcal M_A is the intersection of \Delta^{K-1} with finitely many closed half-spaces and hyperplanes, hence closed in \Delta^{K-1}. A closed subset of a compact set is compact. \square

A.7 Proof of Theorem 3.3 (Existence of Extrema)

Statement. If \mathcal M_A is nonempty and compact, and P \mapsto I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S) is continuous on \Delta^{K-1}, then \Delta_i^-(S;A) and \Delta_i^+(S;A) exist and are attained.

Proof. Continuity of I_P on the finite simplex under the convention 0\log 0 = 0 is a standard result. A continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact set attains its infimum and supremum (Weierstrass). \square

A.8 Proof of the Markov Garbling Condition (Proposition 14.2)

Statement. Consider symmetric binary channels X_1, X_2 \in \{0,1\} with reliabilities

q_i = P(X_i = t \mid T = t), \quad i \in \{1,2\}, \quad t \in \{0,1\},

with q_i > 0.5. The conditional independence T \perp X_1 \mid X_2 is feasible if and only if

q_1 \le q_2.

Proof. Conditional independence T \perp X_1 \mid X_2 requires

P(X_1 = 1 \mid T = t, X_2 = x_2) = P(X_1 = 1 \mid X_2 = x_2)

for all t, x_2. Let

\alpha_2 = P(X_1 = 1 \mid X_2 = 1), \quad \alpha_0 = P(X_1 = 1 \mid X_2 = 0).

Under symmetric reliability and the Markov chain T \to X_2 \to X_1,

P(X_1 = 1 \mid T = 1) = \alpha_2 q_2 + \alpha_0 (1 - q_2) = q_1,

P(X_1 = 1 \mid T = 0) = \alpha_2 (1 - q_2) + \alpha_0 q_2 = 1 - q_1.

Subtracting:

(\alpha_2 - \alpha_0)(2 q_2 - 1) = 2 q_1 - 1,

so

\alpha_2 - \alpha_0 = \frac{2 q_1 - 1}{2 q_2 - 1}.

Adding:

\alpha_2 + \alpha_0 = 1.

Thus

\alpha_2 = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{2 q_1 - 1}{2 (2 q_2 - 1)}, \quad \alpha_0 = \frac{1}{2} - \frac{2 q_1 - 1}{2 (2 q_2 - 1)}.

For \alpha_2, \alpha_0 \in [0,1], we require |\alpha_2 - \alpha_0| \le 1, which holds if and only if

2 q_1 - 1 \le 2 q_2 - 1 \iff q_1 \le q_2.

If q_1 > q_2, the required \alpha_2, \alpha_0 fall outside [0,1], so conditional independence is infeasible. \square

Corollary A.8.1. If q_1 > q_2, then \Delta_1^-(\{2\}; A_4) > 0 strictly. If q_1 \le q_2 and the credal set permits the Markov-degraded channel T \to X_2 \to X_1, then \Delta_1^-(\{2\}; A_4) = 0.

Corollary A.8.2. Adding rung A_5 with conditional independence X_1 \perp X_2 \mid T rules out the Markov chain T \to X_2 \to X_1 and restores \Delta_1^-(\{2\}; A_4 \cap A_5) > 0 for all q_1, q_2 \in (0.5, 1).

A.9 Proof of Proposition C.2 (Monotonicity of Bounds)

Statement. If A_k \subset A_{k+1}, then

\mathcal M_{A_{k+1}} \subseteq \mathcal M_{A_k},

and consequently

\Delta_i^-(S;A_k) \le \Delta_i^-(S;A_{k+1}) \le \Delta_i^+(S;A_{k+1}) \le \Delta_i^+(S;A_k).

Proof. The admissible model space \mathcal M_{A_{k+1}} is the intersection of \mathcal M_{A_k} with the additional constraints in A_{k+1}, hence is a subset. The infimum over a smaller set is at least as large; the supremum over a smaller set is at most as small. \square

Corollary. The identification width W_i(S;A_k) is non-increasing in k.

