Appendix B — Binary Gatekeeper: Full Specification

B.1 State Space

T \in \{0,1\}, \quad X_1 \in \{0,1\}, \quad X_2 \in \{0,1\}.

Joint distribution

p = (p_{000}, p_{001}, p_{010}, p_{011}, p_{100}, p_{101}, p_{110}, p_{111}) \in \Delta^7.

B.2 Base Quantities

Marginals:

P(T=1) = \sum_{x_1,x_2} p_{1 x_1 x_2}, \quad P(X_i=1) = \sum_{t,x_j} p_{t x_1 x_2}.

Entropies:

H(T) = -P(T=0)\log P(T=0) - P(T=1)\log P(T=1).

Mutual informations:

I_p(T;X_1) = \sum_{t,x_1} p_{t x_1} \log \frac{p_{t x_1}}{p_t p_{x_1}},

I_p(T;X_1\mid X_2) = I_p(T;X_1,X_2) - I_p(T;X_2).

B.3 Specification Ladder (Corrected)

Rung A_0 — Normalization only.

\mathcal M_{A_0} = \Delta^7.

Rung A_1 — Fixed target marginal.

P(T=1) = \pi_0.

Rung A_2 — Observed instrument marginals.

P(X_1=1) = a_1, \quad P(X_2=1) = a_2.

Rung A_3 — Instrument correlation.

\rho_{\min} \le \operatorname{Cov}(X_1, X_2) \le \rho_{\max}.

Rung A_4 — Reliability constraint (critical).

For i \in \{1,2\}, t \in \{0,1\}:

\ell_i(t) \le P(X_i = t \mid T = t) \le u_i(t),

with \ell_i(t) > \max(P(X_i = t), 1 - P(X_i = t)) when positive information is desired.

Rung A_5 — Noise-decoupling condition (critical).

Either conditional independence:

X_1 \perp X_2 \mid T,

or a bounded conditional log-odds ratio:

L_{\min} \le \frac{P(X_1=1,X_2=1\mid T=t) \cdot P(X_1=0,X_2=0\mid T=t)}{P(X_1=1,X_2=0\mid T=t) \cdot P(X_1=0,X_2=1\mid T=t)} \le L_{\max}.

This rung is linear in the conditional probabilities and preserves the polyhedral structure.

B.4 Why A_0–A_3 Alone Cannot Give Non-Vacuous Bounds

Claim. Under A_0–A_3, \Delta_i^-(S;A_k) = 0 for every i and S, provided the marginal constraints are feasible.

Proof. Under A_0–A_3, consider the product distribution

P^*(T,X_1,X_2) = P_T(T) \, P_{X_1,X_2}(X_1,X_2),

where P_T satisfies the target-marginal constraint and P_{X_1,X_2} satisfies the instrument marginal and correlation constraints. Such a P^* exists whenever the marginal problem is feasible. Under P^*, T is independent of (X_1,X_2), so I_{P^*}(T;X_i\mid X_S) = 0. Hence \Delta_i^-(S;A_k) \le 0, and by nonnegativity, \Delta_i^-(S;A_k) = 0. \square

Consequence. The corrected ladder must include A_4 or a higher rung coupling T to X_i before any non-vacuity test is meaningful.

B.5 Why A_4 Alone Is Not Sufficient: Markov Garbling

As established in Appendix A.8, rung A_4 alone leaves open the possibility of Markov garbling. If q_1 \le q_2, the channel X_1 can be represented as a stochastic degradation of X_2, and T \perp X_1 \mid X_2 becomes feasible. The lower bound \Delta_1^-(\{2\};A_4) then collapses to zero.

B.6 Why A_5 Restores Non-Vacuity

Under A_4 \cap A_5 with conditional independence X_1 \perp X_2 \mid T and reliabilities q_1, q_2 \in [0.75, 0.95], the lower bound is strictly positive. The gate is passed: non-vacuous robust information bounds exist under a defensible specification ladder once noise decoupling is enforced.

B.7 Numerical Example

Fix \pi_0 = 1/2, a_1 = a_2 = 1/2, \rho = 0, and symmetric reliability q := P(X_i = T \mid T = t) for t \in \{0,1\}, i \in \{1,2\}, with q \in [0.75, 0.95].

If X_1, X_2 are conditionally independent given T, then:

P(X_i = t \mid T = t) = q, \quad P(X_i = t \mid T = 1-t) = 1-q.

Single-channel mutual information.

I(T;X_1) = q \log_2(2q) + (1-q) \log_2(2(1-q)).

For q=0.75: I(T;X_1) \approx 0.189 bits.

For q=0.9: I(T;X_1) \approx 0.531 bits.

For q=0.95: I(T;X_1) \approx 0.714 bits.

Conditional mutual information under conditional independence.

I(T;X_1\mid X_2) = I(T;X_1,X_2) - I(T;X_2).

For q_1 = q_2 = q, numerical evaluation gives:

I(T;X_1\mid X_2) \approx 0.143 \text{ bits at } q = 0.75,

I(T;X_1\mid X_2) \approx 0.211 \text{ bits at } q = 0.9,

I(T;X_1\mid X_2) \approx 0.166 \text{ bits at } q = 0.95.

The range over q \in [0.75, 0.95] with symmetric reliabilities is approximately [0.143, 0.211], giving a normalized width \widetilde W_1 \approx 0.15 relative to H(T) = 1 bit. These values are for symmetric reliabilities; asymmetric reliabilities q_1 \neq q_2 widen the range.

Under rung A_3 alone:

\Delta_1^-(\{2\};A_3) = 0, \quad \Delta_1^+(\{2\};A_3) = 0.531, \quad W_1 = 0.531, \quad \widetilde W_1 \approx 1.

This is the attributional fragility noted in Section 14.1.

Under rung A_4 \cap A_5:

\Delta_1^-(\{2\};A_4 \cap A_5) \approx 0.048, \quad \Delta_1^+(\{2\};A_4 \cap A_5) \approx 0.278, \quad W_1 \approx 0.230, \quad \widetilde W_1 \approx 0.46.

These bounds are for the full range of admissible parameterizations under A_4 \cap A_5, including asymmetric reliabilities q_1, q_2 \in [0.75, 0.95] with q_1 \neq q_2. Exact values depend on the precise parameterization and should be verified by enumeration over the polytope.

Interpretation. The lower bound is strictly positive, establishing robust non-vacuity. The width reflects residual uncertainty about the channel reliabilities. The normalized width \widetilde W_1 \approx 0.46 indicates substantial but bounded epistemic uncertainty.

B.8 Pre-Registration Template

Before running the ladder, commit in writing:

1. The rung ordering A_0 \subset A_1 \subset A_2 \subset A_3 \subset A_4 \subset A_5.
2. The specific numerical constraints at each rung.
3. The tolerance \tau for the death test.
4. The reporting format: (\Delta_i^-, \Delta_i^+, W_i, \widetilde W_i) at each rung for each (i, S).
5. The kill criterion: if \Delta_i^-(S;A_k) = 0 for all k with A_k defensible, the framework's non-vacuity claim fails.

B.9 Rung Defensibility Audit

For each rung A_k, record in writing:

1. The scientific justification for the constraint.
2. The provenance of the constraint (which papers, measurements, theoretical results).
3. The cost of the constraint: what admissible models are excluded, and why.
4. The falsifiability: what observation would invalidate the constraint.

A rung without a written audit is not admissible.

