Appendix D — Kill Operator: Full Specification

D.1 Kill Vector

\mathcal K(c) = (K_{\text{trivial}}, K_{\text{known}}, K_{\text{semantic}}, K_{\text{assumption}}, K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}, K_{\text{unstable}}, K_{\text{extraction}}).

Each component is 0 or 1.

D.2 Definitions of Each Component

K_{\text{trivial}}. Set to 1 if the minimal model \mathcal M^{(0)} produces a trivial phenomenon, defined as:

· T(\mathcal M^{(0)}) is a singleton (no target ambiguity), or
· The contradiction/equivalence structure collapses to a known identity, or
· The refinement induced by candidate c has empty witness set.

K_{\text{known}}. Set to 1 if the literature-collision protocol (Appendix F) returns a formal equivalence with an existing result.

K_{\text{semantic}}. Set to 1 if the apparent contradiction is shown to be a definitional mismatch rather than a substantive incompatibility.

K_{\text{assumption}}. Set to 1 if removing any single assumption A_j from the assumption set A causes the candidate to disappear.

K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}. Set to 1 if no testable prediction, counterexample, or empirical observation can be constructed that would count against the candidate.

K_{\text{unstable}}. Set to 1 if a small perturbation of the model class \mathcal M \to \mathcal M_\epsilon (e.g., adding \epsilon-noise to constraints) causes the candidate to disappear.

K_{\text{extraction}}. Set to 1 if any constraint in the dependency set of c has epistemic status \sigma(k_j) \preceq \text{speculative} or is contradicted or killed.

D.3 Directed Evaluation Graph

The components of \mathcal K(c) are not orthogonal. For example, K_{\text{trivial}}=1 often induces K_{\text{unstable}}=1. The kill operator should therefore be structured as a directed evaluation graph with the following evaluation order:

Stage 1 (fast short-circuit gates):

· K_{\text{extraction}}: check whether any dependency has speculative, contradicted, or killed status.
· K_{\text{semantic}}: check whether the claimed contradiction is definitional.

If either Stage 1 gate fails, the candidate is killed immediately, without further computation. The kill reason is recorded, and evaluation stops.

Stage 2 (structural gates):

· K_{\text{trivial}}: check whether the phenomenon disappears under minimalization.
· K_{\text{known}}: check literature collision.

If either Stage 2 gate fails, the candidate is killed. The kill reason is recorded, and evaluation stops.

Stage 3 (robustness gates):

· K_{\text{assumption}}: check whether the candidate survives removal of any single assumption.
· K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}: check whether a testable prediction exists.
· K_{\text{unstable}}: check stability under perturbation.

Stage 3 gates require the most expensive checks and are evaluated last.

This ordering prevents wasted computation on candidates that fail on trivial or extraction grounds.

D.4 Combination Rule

The kill operator returns a vector, not a scalar. The interpretation:

· \mathcal K(c) = \mathbf{0}: candidate survives all tests.
· Any component equal to 1: candidate fails that test.
· Multiple components equal to 1: candidate fails on multiple grounds.

The framework does not aggregate the components into a scalar. A candidate killed on multiple grounds is not "more killed" than one killed on one ground; the reasons are recorded separately.

D.5 Negative-Knowledge Layer

The set of killed candidates, together with their kill vectors, is

\mathcal K^-(\mathcal A) = \{(c, \mathcal K(c)) : \mathcal K(c) \neq \mathbf{0}\}.

This layer is part of the archive. It can be analyzed for:

· Recurring kill reasons across candidates.
· Recurring assumptions whose removal kills many candidates.
· Recurring extraction bottlenecks.

D.6 Revival Rule

A killed candidate c may be revived if:

1. An archive update removes the specific kill reason. For example, if K_{\text{known}} = 1 because of a paper in the current archive, and that paper is retracted, then the kill reason is removed.
2. A new assumption relaxes the constraint that made K_{\text{assumption}} = 1.
3. New data reduces K_{\text{unstable}} to 0.
4. A status upgrade removes K_{\text{extraction}} = 1.

The revival rule makes the framework monotone in the correct direction: new evidence can un-kill, but only by removing the specific reason for killing.

D.7 Failure as Information

The framework treats failure as information. A killed candidate is not wasted output. It becomes part of the research archive with its kill vector recorded. The negative-knowledge layer \mathcal K^-(\mathcal A) is itself analyzable for structural patterns.

