Appendix E — Provenance and Epistemic Status: Full Specification

E.1 Node-Level Status

Each node v \in V (problem, hypothesis, method, result, concept, question) carries:

\sigma(v) \in \{\text{verified}, \text{supported}, \text{hypothetical}, \text{speculative}, \text{contradicted}, \text{killed}, \text{unresolved}\}.

E.2 Claim-Level Status

Each extracted constraint k_j \in K carries a graded status

\sigma(k_j) \in \{\text{verified}, \text{supported}, \text{hypothetical}, \text{speculative}\},

plus a provenance set \operatorname{prov}(k_j) \subseteq \mathcal A.

E.3 Terminal Status Propagation

If any dependency of k_j has terminal status, the derived status is determined by the absorbent rule (Appendix A.2):

\sigma(k_j) =
\begin{cases}
\text{contradicted} & \text{if any dependency is contradicted} \\
\text{killed} & \text{if any dependency is killed and none contradicted} \\
\text{unresolved} & \text{if any dependency is unresolved and none contradicted/killed} \\
\min_i \sigma(k_{j_i}) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

E.4 Extraction Confidence

Each extraction is accompanied by a confidence score c(k_j) \in [0,1] from the extractor (LLM or rule-based). The confidence is distinct from epistemic status: confidence measures extraction reliability; status measures scientific support.

E.5 Provenance Graph

For each derived object o \in K \cup \mathcal C, define

\operatorname{prov}(o) = \bigcup_{k \in \operatorname{dep}(o)} \operatorname{prov}(k).

The provenance graph \mathcal G_{\text{prov}} has:

· Nodes: documents, claims, candidates.
· Edges: derivation edges.

The provenance graph must be acyclic. If a cycle is detected, the extraction is invalid.

E.6 Audit Query

For any candidate c, the system must answer:

· Which documents contributed to c?
· Which claims are in \operatorname{dep}(c)?
· What is the minimum graded status in \operatorname{dep}(c)?
· Is any dependency contradicted, killed, or unresolved?
· Which rungs of the specification ladder are supported by these claims?

If the minimum graded status is \preceq \text{speculative}, or if any dependency is contradicted or killed, the candidate is killed by K_{\text{extraction}}.

E.7 Status Propagation Example

If paper d_1 (status: verified) supports claim k_1 and paper d_2 (status: hypothetical) supports claim k_2, and candidate c depends on both, then

\sigma(c) = \min(\sigma(k_1), \sigma(k_2)) = \text{hypothetical}.

If the candidate also depends on a speculative extraction k_3, then \sigma(c) = \text{speculative}, and the candidate is killed by K_{\text{extraction}}.

If the candidate depends on a contradicted claim k_4, then \sigma(c) = \text{contradicted}, and the candidate is killed by K_{\text{extraction}}.

