Appendix J — Failure-Mode Catalog

J.1 Extraction Failures

· J.1.1 LLM hallucination. Extracted claim not supported by the source text. Mitigation: require verbatim quote as provenance.
· J.1.2 Semantic drift. Claim extracted with altered meaning. Mitigation: use structured extraction templates; audit samples.
· J.1.3 Coverage gap. Claim present in source but not extracted. Mitigation: use multiple extractors; report inter-extractor agreement.

J.2 Model Failures

· J.2.1 Misspecification. True model outside \Theta. Mitigation: report model-space boundary; test sensitivity.
· J.2.2 Over-parameterization. \Theta too rich; \mathcal M not informative. Mitigation: minimal-model principle.
· J.2.3 Under-parameterization. \Theta too poor; no model fits. Mitigation: expand \Theta; re-extract.

J.3 Tension Failures

· J.3.1 MIS explosion. Too many minimal inconsistent subsets. Mitigation: bound |S|; use MDL-gap.
· J.3.2 Semantic false contradiction. Definitional mismatch. Mitigation: semantic equivalence clustering before MIS detection.
· J.3.3 Trivial contradiction. Contradiction exists but is uninteresting. Mitigation: require tension to be target-relevant.

J.4 Candidate Failures

· J.4.1 Trivial candidate. Phenomenon disappears under minimalization. Mitigation: minimal-model gate.
· J.4.2 Known candidate. Already in literature. Mitigation: collision protocol.
· J.4.3 Non-falsifiable candidate. No test possible. Mitigation: kill by K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}.

J.5 Information Failures

· J.5.1 \Delta_c^- = 0 pathology. Reported as non-vacuous. Mitigation: report both endpoints; require \Delta_c^- > 0 for robust non-vacuity.
· J.5.2 Information-value mismatch. High CMI but trivial target. Mitigation: Pareto ranking includes identification gain G separately.
· J.5.3 Numerical instability. Nonlinear optimization fails. Mitigation: branch-and-bound with certificates.
· J.5.4 Markov garbling. Lower bound collapses to zero through channel degradation. Mitigation: include noise-decoupling rung in specification ladder.

J.6 Evaluation Failures

· J.6.1 Benchmark contamination. Post-t_0 information leaks. Mitigation: leakage protocol (Appendix G.2).
· J.6.2 Evaluator bias. Evaluators know the ground truth. Mitigation: blind evaluation.
· J.6.3 Negative-control failure. Method produces candidates from noise. Mitigation: report negative-control results; pre-register controls.

J.7 Epistemic Failures

· J.7.1 Status collapse. Speculative output treated as verified. Mitigation: status layer (Appendix E).
· J.7.2 Provenance loss. Candidate without traceable provenance. Mitigation: provenance graph; audit query (Appendix E.6).
· J.7.3 LLM convergence illusion. Multiple LLMs agree but share training bias. Mitigation: treat agreement as stability evidence, not truth evidence.
· J.7.4 Terminal-status neglect. Contradicted or killed premises do not propagate. Mitigation: terminal absorbent rules (Appendix A.2).

J.8 Complexity Failures

· J.8.1 MIS NP-hardness. Exhaustive enumeration infeasible. Mitigation: bound |S|; use incremental solvers.
· J.8.2 Equivalence undecidability. Rich formal languages. Mitigation: restrict to decidable fragments.
· J.8.3 Pareto frontier explosion. Too many non-dominated candidates. Mitigation: report top-k by a specified tie-breaker; document the tie-breaker.

J.9 Causal Failures

· J.9.1 Observational-interventional confusion. A candidate that is only an observation cannot distinguish models that are observationally equivalent but interventionally distinct. Mitigation: carry both observational and interventional layers.
· J.9.2 Unmeasured confounding. Interventional equivalence may still fail to identify the target under unmeasured confounding. Mitigation: report bounds, not point estimates.

