Appendix N — Causal and Interventional Extension

N.1 Observational Versus Interventional Equivalence

For passive observations, the equivalence relation is

\theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2 \iff P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B) \quad \forall B.

For interventions, the equivalence relation is

\theta_1 \sim_X^{\mathrm{do}} \theta_2 \iff P_{\theta_1}(X\in B \mid \mathrm{do}(a)) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B \mid \mathrm{do}(a)) \quad \forall B, a.

The interventional equivalence relation is finer than the observational one:

\sim_X^{\mathrm{do}} \subseteq \sim_X.

N.2 Interventional Refinement Theorem

Theorem N.1. Let \theta_1, \theta_2 \in \mathcal M such that \theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2 but \theta_1 \not\sim_X^{\mathrm{do}} \theta_2. Then the intervention a that witnesses the interventional distinction strictly refines the observational equivalence class:

[\theta_1]^{\mathrm{do}} \subsetneq [\theta_1]_X.

Proof. By assumption there exists an intervention a and a measurable B such that

P_{\theta_1}(X\in B \mid \mathrm{do}(a)) \neq P_{\theta_2}(X\in B \mid \mathrm{do}(a)).

Therefore \theta_2 \notin [\theta_1]^{\mathrm{do}}. Since \theta_2 \in [\theta_1]_X, the inclusion is proper. \square

N.3 Causal Candidate Generation

When the candidate set \mathfrak C(\mathcal M) includes interventions, candidate generation must search over:

· Passive observations.
· Controlled manipulations.
· Instrumental variables.
· Natural experiments.
· Quasi-experimental designs.

Each candidate type induces a different refinement operator \mathfrak R^{\mathrm{obs}} or \mathfrak R^{\mathrm{do}}. The framework must track which type is used for each candidate, since observational and interventional refinements are not interchangeable.

N.4 Unmeasured Confounding

Under unmeasured confounding, the interventional distribution P_\theta(Y \mid \mathrm{do}(a)) may not be point-identified from observational data alone. The framework inherits the partial-identification bounds of causal inference:

P_\theta(Y \mid \mathrm{do}(a)) \in [\underline P, \overline P].

The robust information bounds \Delta_c^- and \Delta_c^+ must then be computed over the causal credal set, not the observational one. This is a strictly harder problem than the purely observational case.

N.5 Open Problem

Characterize the relationship between observational refinement and interventional refinement. Under what conditions does the interventional layer strictly enlarge the set of distinguishable model pairs? Under what conditions does it collapse to the observational layer?